4,\n  \"stats\": {\n    \"shots\": 534,\n    \"hits\": 197,\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_81","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat > README.md <<'EOF'\n# Buzz Comb Party\n\nAn original six-garden arcade shooter inspired by Buzz Bombers (1983). Spray bees into combs, use those combs to redirect workers, feed them to Pip the hummingbird, conserve cans and keep the flowers out of your parking space. Red harvests open the pageant's shield buds. The ordinary-input automated solo/co-op campaigns take about 10½ minutes.\n\nOpen the final HTML one directory above this folder. It is self-contained, works with \"'`file://`, has no runtime network dependencies, and may be moved anywhere. The folder name and final basename follow TASK.md. `exit-interview.json` beside the final HTML supplies catalogue/release metadata.\n\n- `npm run build` reproduces the HTML from these sources and the current interview.\n- `npm test` runs the quiet aggregate checks; only failures and one final count are printed. `npm test -- sim`, `-- browser`, `-- hub` or `-- build` selects a group.\n- Tests use Node and the workstation'\"'s existing Playwright Python 1.63 environment. No dependency install, browser download or development server is needed for standalone. The Hub test starts and shuts down its own loopback HTTP server on an OS-assigned port.\n\nControls: A/D or arrows move, Space sprays, E calls Pip, Escape pauses. Mouse movement chooses a column and holding left sprays; right calls. Standard pad: left stick / D-pad, Cross or R2 spray, Square call, Options pause, Circle back. PlayStation symbols are default; Xbox is in Controls. All native key/button bindings can be changed.\n\nLocal co-op offers keyboard + pad, two pads, or one shared keyboard. Shared keyboard: P1 A/D, Space, E; P2 arrows, period, slash. Both cans share score, stock and bird cooldown. The setup assigns pads exclusively. Online shared-world 1–2 player co-op is supplied by AI Hub; there is no standalone room broker. Online Options is local and the shared world continues.\n\nTouch: drag the lower garden; safe assisted fire avoids Pip and valuable wax. Tap a comb for contextual Harvest / Clear, or cancel. Clear uses normal travel and ammo. The native menu button remains available; portrait and landscape keep the same collision arena.\n\nAfter ten idle seconds on the front end, a looping simulation showcase starts. Any input returns to title and is consumed. Attract and assisted runs cannot write ordinary records.\n\nPause → Developer Tools opens controller-navigable encounter/phase selection, single stepping, freeze/slow motion, stock protection, unlimited spray, collision/ledger inspection, object spawning, live tuning/reset and validated JSON export/import. Mutation marks a run assisted. Timing changes affect existing motion immediately; entry timing is baked on encounter start/restart. New normal runs always use defaults. Online sessions expose local options only.\n\nAuthor content in \"'`src/content.js`; see `docs/content-guide.md`. The rendering-independent simulation, wire format and ownership rules are in `docs/contracts.md`. Research/provenance, engine patches, distinct test-discovered issues and full evidence are in `docs/` and `evidence/`.\n\nVerification limits: controller and touch inputs are emulated. Chromium/WebKit are browser checks, not physical iOS. Hub conformance uses the official local coordinator with real game frames, not the live catalogue. Nobody listened to the generated audio; objective signal checks cover it. Human difficulty/fairness remains unconfirmed. No usage/cost estimate is made; metadata says “added on import”.\nEOF\ncat > docs/content-guide.md <<'\"'EOF'\n# Authoring the garden\n\nContent is ordinary JavaScript data in \"'`src/content.js`. The runtime accepts the same data as JSON via Pause → Developer Tools → Export / import. `BCP.validateContent(array)` validates before replacing a known-good world. Import takes `{version:1,tuning:{...},content:[...]}`; omit content for tuning only. A successful content import restarts the current garden and marks the run assisted. Invalid types, non-finite numbers, duplicate identities, out-of-arena positions, bad palettes and out-of-range phase conditions are rejected without applying a partial import.\n\nEach encounter defines a stable `id`, `name`, short `sub`, `lesson` and success `gag`; three hex palette colors; a worker speed multiplier; `clearBonus`; initial ground `flowers` as blocked intervals; `combs` as `{id,x,tier,red}`; and timeline `entries` as `{id,at,kind,x,dir,tier}`. `kind` is `worker` or `fast`, tier is 0–6, x is 28–612, direction ±1 and `at` is seconds after the opening card. An optional `growth` array has `{at,x0,x1}`. Optional `boss` defines phases, hp, exposure seconds and repeat supply seconds. The pageant has one or three phases; maximum three.\n\nThe can has radius 10 plus two pixels clearance. Keep at least one wide legal ground interval. Flowers may split the rail, but one garden must never begin fully enclosed. The native fill/expand setting may change decoration, never coordinates. Palette changes are rendering data only. Score bands are defined by `feedValue` in simulation: 1,000 / 800 / 500 amber from high to low, plus 1,000 for red. Hit rewards are 100 worker and 1,000 express; hive/clear rewards are claimed once per stable identity.\n\n## Example: add a normal wave\n\nExport a diagnostic JSON, keep its `content`, and append these entries to encounter 2'\"'s \"'`entries`:\n\n```json\n[\n  {\"id\":\"after-tea-a\",\"at\":90,\"kind\":\"worker\",\"x\":40,\"dir\":1,\"tier\":1},\n  {\"id\":\"after-tea-b\",\"at\":93,\"kind\":\"fast\",\"x\":600,\"dir\":-1,\"tier\":0}\n]\n```\n\nImport the JSON. The pending-entry completion predicate naturally waits for them. A new arrangement requires no simulation changes; the simulation suite exercises adding an entry using this path. Reuse the encounter'\"'s palette/flower layout, or edit those fields in the same profile. Every entry ID must be unique inside its encounter so retries retain reward identity.\n\n## Example: alter a finale phase\n\nChange the final encounter's \"'`boss` data to:\n\n```json\n{\"phases\":3,\"hp\":7,\"exposure\":18,\"supply\":9}\n```\n\nThis gives each of the three buds seven spray hits and an 18-second window after every red harvest, with recurring express opportunities every nine seconds. Phase entry resets HP and exposure, and shortens the next supply delay. All three phases use the same fields by design; palette/animation varies by phase. The final phase schedules four mixed curtain-call entries after the last bud. Rescue escorts keep supplying combs after eight finite reward identities per phase; later repeats cannot manufacture points or reserve stock.\n\nRun `npm test -- sim` after a data edit. It must still finish the full campaign with ordinary ammo/stock/cooldowns and balance its ledger. The bot'\"'s precision is not evidence of fair human difficulty; preserve first-window generosity and visible warnings.\n\n## Tuning and debugging policy\n\nImportant values and documented units are in \"'`src/tuning.js` and in the native slider panels. A tuning import merges into safe defaults and validates all values. Existing bee/can movement uses current values immediately. Existing entry times remain fixed until encounter restart, because changing deadlines while a wave is underway is hard to interpret. Capacity changes clamp current fill. Reset all tuning resets both widgets and actual values. Settings/profiles are separate from normal run defaults.\n\nA hive pocket needs a same-tier open corridor narrower than 35 pixels for the full construction delay. The overlay shows the exact corridor reason. A comb on another tier cannot falsely trap the bee. Friendly bird fire, cleared targets and removal of pocket boundaries all recover automatically.\nEOF\ncat > docs/test-report.md <<'\"'EOF'\n# Verification record\n\nThe one-command suite is \"'`npm test`, orchestrated by `tools/test.mjs`. Quiet groups are simulation, assembled browser/input/audio, official Hub plus two-copy behavior, and packaging/schema. Failure details are printed; passing cases produce only the final count. Detailed reports are written under `evidence/`.\n\nSimulation includes swept two-hit spray/newborn exclusion, travel-order obstacle consumption, atomic worker routes, express pass-through, real hive pockets and cancellation, deterministic free cells, bird call/cooldown/target invalidation/feeding/retreat/return, exact shot costs, swapping, bounded grace, last-shot clear priority, last-harvest stock recovery, multi-threshold awards, finite retry/rescue rewards, flower intervals/hops/enclosure batching, delayed-entry clear, data authoring, reproducibility and wire packing/validation. Native headless title/play/pause/resume is exercised with drawing enabled.\n\nFull ordinary-input pilot uses `BCP.applyAction` at 10 Hz and bounded can movement with normal stock, ammo, fire cadence, feed timing and cooldown. It never deletes actors, teleports, changes phases or enables developer protection. It completes the six gardens in order, converts workers/express bees, preserves/banks a hive, harvests red and amber, clears obstructing wax, handles authored flower pressure and solves all four shield buds. Solo/co-op results are saved in `playthrough-1p.json` and `playthrough-2p.json`. A whole campaign also runs inside the assembled browser artifact and reaches its real win/results/restart flow.\n\nBrowser checks use actual final HTML via file://, Chromium and WebKit. Standard pads are stubbed; touch uses mobile device emulation and real CDP touch events, not physical hardware. Tests cover controller navigation/move/fire/call/pause, keyboard/mouse ownership, records/settings, live tuning/reset/import/step, ten-second attract and consumed wakes, exclusive local co-op devices, touch assist/context, orientation and error-free canvas draws. Screenshots are for composition only: title, busy wave plus flower warning, finale, pause/dev, results, About and touch.\n\nAudio uses native code synthesis and an original procedural garden scherzo with generated gibberish voices. OfflineAudioContext renders the shared oscillator/envelope code, verifies finite non-silent output, no clipping, ten-percent gain ratio and exact mute silence. Teardown is inspected. No human listened; musical quality is unconfirmed.\n\nOfficial pinned harness fixtures are unchanged and hashed. The wrapper runs two players, latency=150/jitter=50/loss=5, host-leave, server-host and one-player cases. The harness'\"'s completion flag and pass result are required. Additional real-copy checks exercise simultaneous spray/call, shared bee conversion/score, non-pausing online options, malformed input rejection, enclosure reset agreement, departure removal without stock loss, a single host finish, and no world advancement or sends after end. Production Hub/catalogue integration remains untested. Test servers bind 127.0.0.1 on an OS-assigned port and are shut down by the owning test.\n\nDistinct implementation issues and critical classification are tracked in \"'`docs/issues.json`; assertion/fixture mistakes are not inflated into product issue counts. The strongest findings came from actual gameplay-copy networking and visual inspection; boot success alone did not reveal missing actor crops. A phone debug script initially returned the entire native Game object to Playwright, causing expensive serialization; tests now return compact values. That was a test artifact, not a game defect.\n\nKnown limits: no physical controller, real touch device, iPhone/iPad, TV, human audio review or human balance playtest. The ordinary-input bots demonstrate reachability and accounting, not subjective first-run fairness. No FPS/performance target was introduced.\nEOF'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_81","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat > README.md <<'EOF'\n# Buzz Comb Party\n\nAn original six-garden arcade shooter inspired by Buzz Bombers (1983). Spray bees into combs, use those combs to redirect workers, feed them to Pip the hummingbird, conserve cans and keep the flowers out of your parking space. Red harvests open the pageant's shield buds. The ordinary-input automated solo/co-op campaigns take about 10½ minutes.\n\nOpen the final HTML one directory above this folder. It is self-contained, works with \"'`file://`, has no runtime network dependencies, and may be moved anywhere. The folder name and final basename follow TASK.md. `exit-interview.json` beside the final HTML supplies catalogue/release metadata.\n\n- `npm run build` reproduces the HTML from these sources and the current interview.\n- `npm test` runs the quiet aggregate checks; only failures and one final count are printed. `npm test -- sim`, `-- browser`, `-- hub` or `-- build` selects a group.\n- Tests use Node and the workstation'\"'s existing Playwright Python 1.63 environment. No dependency install, browser download or development server is needed for standalone. The Hub test starts and shuts down its own loopback HTTP server on an OS-assigned port.\n\nControls: A/D or arrows move, Space sprays, E calls Pip, Escape pauses. Mouse movement chooses a column and holding left sprays; right calls. Standard pad: left stick / D-pad, Cross or R2 spray, Square call, Options pause, Circle back. PlayStation symbols are default; Xbox is in Controls. All native key/button bindings can be changed.\n\nLocal co-op offers keyboard + pad, two pads, or one shared keyboard. Shared keyboard: P1 A/D, Space, E; P2 arrows, period, slash. Both cans share score, stock and bird cooldown. The setup assigns pads exclusively. Online shared-world 1–2 player co-op is supplied by AI Hub; there is no standalone room broker. Online Options is local and the shared world continues.\n\nTouch: drag the lower garden; safe assisted fire avoids Pip and valuable wax. Tap a comb for contextual Harvest / Clear, or cancel. Clear uses normal travel and ammo. The native menu button remains available; portrait and landscape keep the same collision arena.\n\nAfter ten idle seconds on the front end, a looping simulation showcase starts. Any input returns to title and is consumed. Attract and assisted runs cannot write ordinary records.\n\nPause → Developer Tools opens controller-navigable encounter/phase selection, single stepping, freeze/slow motion, stock protection, unlimited spray, collision/ledger inspection, object spawning, live tuning/reset and validated JSON export/import. Mutation marks a run assisted. Timing changes affect existing motion immediately; entry timing is baked on encounter start/restart. New normal runs always use defaults. Online sessions expose local options only.\n\nAuthor content in \"'`src/content.js`; see `docs/content-guide.md`. The rendering-independent simulation, wire format and ownership rules are in `docs/contracts.md`. Research/provenance, engine patches, distinct test-discovered issues and full evidence are in `docs/` and `evidence/`.\n\nVerification limits: controller and touch inputs are emulated. Chromium/WebKit are browser checks, not physical iOS. Hub conformance uses the official local coordinator with real game frames, not the live catalogue. Nobody listened to the generated audio; objective signal checks cover it. Human difficulty/fairness remains unconfirmed. No usage/cost estimate is made; metadata says “added on import”.\nEOF\ncat > docs/content-guide.md <<'\"'EOF'\n# Authoring the garden\n\nContent is ordinary JavaScript data in \"'`src/content.js`. The runtime accepts the same data as JSON via Pause → Developer Tools → Export / import. `BCP.validateContent(array)` validates before replacing a known-good world. Import takes `{version:1,tuning:{...},content:[...]}`; omit content for tuning only. A successful content import restarts the current garden and marks the run assisted. Invalid types, non-finite numbers, duplicate identities, out-of-arena positions, bad palettes and out-of-range phase conditions are rejected without applying a partial import.\n\nEach encounter defines a stable `id`, `name`, short `sub`, `lesson` and success `gag`; three hex palette colors; a worker speed multiplier; `clearBonus`; initial ground `flowers` as blocked intervals; `combs` as `{id,x,tier,red}`; and timeline `entries` as `{id,at,kind,x,dir,tier}`. `kind` is `worker` or `fast`, tier is 0–6, x is 28–612, direction ±1 and `at` is seconds after the opening card. An optional `growth` array has `{at,x0,x1}`. Optional `boss` defines phases, hp, exposure seconds and repeat supply seconds. The pageant has one or three phases; maximum three.\n\nThe can has radius 10 plus two pixels clearance. Keep at least one wide legal ground interval. Flowers may split the rail, but one garden must never begin fully enclosed. The native fill/expand setting may change decoration, never coordinates. Palette changes are rendering data only. Score bands are defined by `feedValue` in simulation: 1,000 / 800 / 500 amber from high to low, plus 1,000 for red. Hit rewards are 100 worker and 1,000 express; hive/clear rewards are claimed once per stable identity.\n\n## Example: add a normal wave\n\nExport a diagnostic JSON, keep its `content`, and append these entries to encounter 2'\"'s \"'`entries`:\n\n```json\n[\n  {\"id\":\"after-tea-a\",\"at\":90,\"kind\":\"worker\",\"x\":40,\"dir\":1,\"tier\":1},\n  {\"id\":\"after-tea-b\",\"at\":93,\"kind\":\"fast\",\"x\":600,\"dir\":-1,\"tier\":0}\n]\n```\n\nImport the JSON. The pending-entry completion predicate naturally waits for them. A new arrangement requires no simulation changes; the simulation suite exercises adding an entry using this path. Reuse the encounter'\"'s palette/flower layout, or edit those fields in the same profile. Every entry ID must be unique inside its encounter so retries retain reward identity.\n\n## Example: alter a finale phase\n\nChange the final encounter's \"'`boss` data to:\n\n```json\n{\"phases\":3,\"hp\":7,\"exposure\":18,\"supply\":9}\n```\n\nThis gives each of the three buds seven spray hits and an 18-second window after every red harvest, with recurring express opportunities every nine seconds. Phase entry resets HP and exposure, and shortens the next supply delay. All three phases use the same fields by design; palette/animation varies by phase. The final phase schedules four mixed curtain-call entries after the last bud. Rescue escorts keep supplying combs after eight finite reward identities per phase; later repeats cannot manufacture points or reserve stock.\n\nRun `npm test -- sim` after a data edit. It must still finish the full campaign with ordinary ammo/stock/cooldowns and balance its ledger. The bot'\"'s precision is not evidence of fair human difficulty; preserve first-window generosity and visible warnings.\n\n## Tuning and debugging policy\n\nImportant values and documented units are in \"'`src/tuning.js` and in the native slider panels. A tuning import merges into safe defaults and validates all values. Existing bee/can movement uses current values immediately. Existing entry times remain fixed until encounter restart, because changing deadlines while a wave is underway is hard to interpret. Capacity changes clamp current fill. Reset all tuning resets both widgets and actual values. Settings/profiles are separate from normal run defaults.\n\nA hive pocket needs a same-tier open corridor narrower than 35 pixels for the full construction delay. The overlay shows the exact corridor reason. A comb on another tier cannot falsely trap the bee. Friendly bird fire, cleared targets and removal of pocket boundaries all recover automatically.\nEOF\ncat > docs/test-report.md <<'\"'EOF'\n# Verification record\n\nThe one-command suite is \"'`npm test`, orchestrated by `tools/test.mjs`. Quiet groups are simulation, assembled browser/input/audio, official Hub plus two-copy behavior, and packaging/schema. Failure details are printed; passing cases produce only the final count. Detailed reports are written under `evidence/`.\n\nSimulation includes swept two-hit spray/newborn exclusion, travel-order obstacle consumption, atomic worker routes, express pass-through, real hive pockets and cancellation, deterministic free cells, bird call/cooldown/target invalidation/feeding/retreat/return, exact shot costs, swapping, bounded grace, last-shot clear priority, last-harvest stock recovery, multi-threshold awards, finite retry/rescue rewards, flower intervals/hops/enclosure batching, delayed-entry clear, data authoring, reproducibility and wire packing/validation. Native headless title/play/pause/resume is exercised with drawing enabled.\n\nFull ordinary-input pilot uses `BCP.applyAction` at 10 Hz and bounded can movement with normal stock, ammo, fire cadence, feed timing and cooldown. It never deletes actors, teleports, changes phases or enables developer protection. It completes the six gardens in order, converts workers/express bees, preserves/banks a hive, harvests red and amber, clears obstructing wax, handles authored flower pressure and solves all four shield buds. Solo/co-op results are saved in `playthrough-1p.json` and `playthrough-2p.json`. A whole campaign also runs inside the assembled browser artifact and reaches its real win/results/restart flow.\n\nBrowser checks use actual final HTML via file://, Chromium and WebKit. Standard pads are stubbed; touch uses mobile device emulation and real CDP touch events, not physical hardware. Tests cover controller navigation/move/fire/call/pause, keyboard/mouse ownership, records/settings, live tuning/reset/import/step, ten-second attract and consumed wakes, exclusive local co-op devices, touch assist/context, orientation and error-free canvas draws. Screenshots are for composition only: title, busy wave plus flower warning, finale, pause/dev, results, About and touch.\n\nAudio uses native code synthesis and an original procedural garden scherzo with generated gibberish voices. OfflineAudioContext renders the shared oscillator/envelope code, verifies finite non-silent output, no clipping, ten-percent gain ratio and exact mute silence. Teardown is inspected. No human listened; musical quality is unconfirmed.\n\nOfficial pinned harness fixtures are unchanged and hashed. The wrapper runs two players, latency=150/jitter=50/loss=5, host-leave, server-host and one-player cases. The harness'\"'s completion flag and pass result are required. Additional real-copy checks exercise simultaneous spray/call, shared bee conversion/score, non-pausing online options, malformed input rejection, enclosure reset agreement, departure removal without stock loss, a single host finish, and no world advancement or sends after end. Production Hub/catalogue integration remains untested. Test servers bind 127.0.0.1 on an OS-assigned port and are shut down by the owning test.\n\nDistinct implementation issues and critical classification are tracked in \"'`docs/issues.json`; assertion/fixture mistakes are not inflated into product issue counts. The strongest findings came from actual gameplay-copy networking and visual inspection; boot success alone did not reveal missing actor crops. A phone debug script initially returned the entire native Game object to Playwright, causing expensive serialization; tests now return compact values. That was a test artifact, not a game defect.\n\nKnown limits: no physical controller, real touch device, iPhone/iPad, TV, human audio review or human balance playtest. The ordinary-input bots demonstrate reachability and accounting, not subjective first-run fairness. No FPS/performance target was introduced.\nEOF'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
